# OpenClaw — MCP Training Loop

## Purpose

Turn every governance decision an operator makes over an MCP tool call into
a **preference pair** that eventually fine-tunes a router policy. The
router policy selects which MCP to trust for a given task at request time.

This is the ZIE double-dip flywheel pattern (see `04-model-forge.mdc`)
extended into the MCP surface. The differentiator is that the training corpus
is *cross-MCP governance labels* — a dataset neither Anthropic nor OpenAI has,
because it requires a neutral registry that spans multiple MCP providers.

## Loop

```
                ┌──────────────────────────┐
Operator ──▶ MCP tool call ──▶ recordInvocation() ──▶ buffer/JSONL
                │                                          │
                │                                          ▼
                │                              Governance / operator labels
                │                              via /invocations/:id/label
                │                                          │
                │                                          ▼
                │                       verifiedPairCounts() per (mcp, tool)
                │                                          │
                │                              threshold hit? (25 verified, 25 safe, 25 unsafe)
                │                                          │
                │                                          ▼
                │                              checkAndDispatch() ─▶ Modal LoRA
                │                                          │
                │                                          ▼
                │                              updatePolicy() writes preferred_mcp_slug
                │                                          │
                ▼                                          ▼
        Next tool call routes ─────────── getPolicy(task_hint)
```

## Storage

- **In-memory buffer** — `BUFFER: McpPreferencePair[]` in `trainingLoop.ts`
- **Disk mirror** — appends to `/tmp/mcp-training-records.jsonl` (configurable via `MCP_TRAINING_BUFFER_PATH`)
- **DB (planned)** — `mcp_training_records` and `mcp_router_policies` tables. Not yet migrated in this branch — see §Schema below.

The in-memory buffer + disk mirror is intentional: the loop must survive a
DRY_RUN local dev environment with no DB. When the DB is provisioned, add
a drizzle write path in `recordInvocation()` and `labelPair()` alongside the
buffer.

## Thresholds

| Signal | Value | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `VERIFIED_PAIR_THRESHOLD` | 25 per (slug, tool) | Minimum verified pairs before firing training |
| `MIN_SFT_PAIRS` | 100 (planned) | Minimum SFT rows required by Modal trainer |
| `MIN_DPO_PAIRS` | 25 | Minimum DPO pairs required by Modal trainer |

## HTTP contract

Mounted at `/api/mcps/training/*`:

| Method | Path | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/health` | Buffer size + path |
| POST | `/invocations` | Record an MCP tool invocation |
| POST | `/invocations/:id/label` | Label safe/unsafe/defer |
| GET | `/pairs` | Verified-pair counts per (mcp, tool) |
| POST | `/check-thresholds` | Trigger threshold check + dispatch |
| GET | `/policies` | List current router policies |
| POST | `/policies` | Upsert a router policy |
| POST | `/eval` | Inspection-only eval before promoting |

## Modal integration

Same DRY_RUN convention as `modalDispatch.ts` — `DRY_RUN=1` returns stub
functionCallIds without contacting Modal. Live path spawns
`modal.functions.fromName(MCP_MODAL_FUNCTION, "train_lora").spawn(...)` with
`(mcp_slug, tool_name, pairs)` payload.

Env vars:
- `DRY_RUN` — set to `1` for stub dispatch
- `MCP_MODAL_FUNCTION` — Modal app name (default `mcp-router-trainer`)
- `MCP_TRAINING_BUFFER_PATH` — override the disk buffer path

## Router policy

`MCPRouterPolicy = { task_hint, preferred_mcp_slug, preferred_tool_name, candidate_mcp_slugs, updated_at, based_on_pairs }`.
Currently in-memory; replace `POLICIES` Map with a drizzle write against
`mcp_router_policies` when the DB schema lands.

## Schema (planned)

Not yet migrated in this branch — sketch:

```sql
CREATE TABLE mcp_training_records (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  mcp_slug TEXT NOT NULL,
  tool_name TEXT NOT NULL,
  invocation JSONB NOT NULL,
  label TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'defer',
  reason TEXT,
  labelled_by TEXT,
  labelled_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  used_for_training BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE,
  invoked_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  operator_id TEXT,
  tenant_id INTEGER
);

CREATE TABLE mcp_router_policies (
  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  task_hint TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  preferred_mcp_slug TEXT NOT NULL,
  preferred_tool_name TEXT NOT NULL,
  candidate_mcp_slugs TEXT[] NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',
  based_on_pairs INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
```

## What NOT to do

- Do NOT auto-label pairs. Label is a human/governance signal only.
- Do NOT include the raw invocation output in fine-tune training data
  without a redaction pass. Some tool outputs contain PII / secrets that
  the MCP forwarded from its downstream system.
- Do NOT flip a router policy without calling `/eval` first — the eval
  cross-checks the gate grade of the candidate MCP.
- Do NOT extend this loop to *arbitrary* skill-generation training data —
  that belongs in the Archon L0-L4 pipeline. This loop is MCP-scoped.
